WEBVTT

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:04.108
Mia, I brought a balloon, a little
blood cell model, and a sugar cube.

00:00:04.442 --> 00:00:05.814
Which one explains breathing?

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.231
The balloon.

00:00:07.727 --> 00:00:10.458
My lungs inflate themselves
like a birthday balloon.

00:00:11.029 --> 00:00:12.625
Party inside my chest.

00:00:13.399 --> 00:00:15.734
A party with a serious misconception.

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:18.966
Your lungs do not inflate
themselves with a pump muscle.

00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:22.963
Your diaphragm and muscles between
the ribs change the space around them.

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:25.110
We will see why air then moves.

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:28.490
And the sugar cube is an emergency snack?

00:00:29.256 --> 00:00:30.756
It represents glucose.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:33.074
Breathing moves air in and out.

00:00:33.449 --> 00:00:37.150
Gas exchange moves oxygen from
the air into the blood and

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.809
carbon dioxide the other way.

00:00:39.315 --> 00:00:43.873
Respiration is the chemical process in
cells that releases energy from food.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.144
Three names, three jobs: air
movement, gas swap, chemical reaction.

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:51.284
Good.

00:00:51.721 --> 00:00:52.920
Our board connects them.

00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:57.285
Breathing brings fresh air to the
lungs, gas exchange puts oxygen

00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:01.217
into blood, and blood carries it
to cells for aerobic respiration.

00:01:01.626 --> 00:01:04.059
Carbon dioxide travels back for removal.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.612
So if I write, "Respiration happens
when my lungs fill up," you might

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:11.378
shed an imaginary teacher tear?

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:13.086
A tiny one.

00:01:13.401 --> 00:01:16.065
In an exam, that could
lose the distinction mark.

00:01:16.272 --> 00:01:19.866
Write that respiration
releases energy in cells.

00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:24.113
Breathing and respiration are connected,
but they are not the same process.

00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:28.015
Does respiration stop during
a short pause between breaths?

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:29.178
No.

00:01:29.696 --> 00:01:34.345
Cells keep working using oxygen already
available in the blood and tissues.

00:01:34.752 --> 00:01:37.462
We do not need a breath-holding
contest to prove it.

00:01:37.824 --> 00:01:39.767
Normal breathing is our model today.

00:01:40.523 --> 00:01:44.611
The sugar cube powers the cells, and
the lungs run the delivery service.

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:49.633
Energy is released from glucose, not
created, and our delivery service

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.535
also collects carbon dioxide.

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.149
Let's follow one oxygen molecule.

00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:57.463
Our oxygen molecule enters
through the nose or mouth.

00:01:57.887 --> 00:02:01.413
In the nose, air is warmed,
moistened, and filtered.

00:02:01.745 --> 00:02:04.331
Then it travels down
the trachea or windpipe.

00:02:05.196 --> 00:02:06.392
A floppy drinking straw?

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.270
Those sometimes collapse
when I suck too hard.

00:02:10.038 --> 00:02:11.309
A useful comparison.

00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:14.309
Rings of cartilage help
keep the trachea open.

00:02:14.754 --> 00:02:18.059
It divides into two bronchi,
one entering each lung.

00:02:18.642 --> 00:02:21.224
Each bronchus branches
into smaller bronchioles.

00:02:22.028 --> 00:02:24.588
Trachea, bronchi, bronchioles.

00:02:25.022 --> 00:02:26.840
The names get smaller with the tubes.

00:02:27.598 --> 00:02:30.531
And at the ends are tiny
air sacs called alveoli.

00:02:30.972 --> 00:02:32.518
One is an alveolus.

00:02:32.865 --> 00:02:36.495
Their huge combined surface is
where oxygen and carbon dioxide

00:02:36.495 --> 00:02:38.098
cross between air and blood.

00:02:38.859 --> 00:02:42.072
So the trachea is not where
the main gas swap happens.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:43.323
It is the route there.

00:02:44.081 --> 00:02:44.908
Exactly.

00:02:45.328 --> 00:02:48.925
Trace the route on the board:
nose or mouth, trachea,

00:02:49.191 --> 00:02:52.042
bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli.

00:02:52.537 --> 00:02:55.792
Air returns along the same branching
route when you breathe out.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.216
It is not a one-way conveyor belt.

00:02:59.025 --> 00:03:02.043
What stops dust reaching
the delicate air sacs?

00:03:02.787 --> 00:03:05.424
Sticky mucus traps
particles in the airways.

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:07.621
Goblet cells produce mucus.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.630
Ciliated cells have tiny hair-like
structures called cilia that move

00:03:12.653 --> 00:03:15.374
the mucus towards the throat,
where it can be swallowed.

00:03:16.073 --> 00:03:18.063
Goblet cells make the sticky carpet.

00:03:18.371 --> 00:03:19.363
Cilia move it.

00:03:19.693 --> 00:03:21.778
A very unglamorous escalator.

00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:23.843
Beautifully useful though.

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:26.956
Around the lungs, ribs protect the chest.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.342
Intercostal muscles lie between the ribs.

00:03:29.641 --> 00:03:34.169
The diaphragm is a sheet of muscle below
the lungs, not a pipe carrying air.

00:03:34.956 --> 00:03:37.231
And those thin layers
around the lung model?

00:03:37.987 --> 00:03:40.982
Pleural membranes with a thin
layer of fluid between them.

00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:45.007
They reduce friction and help couple the
lungs to movements of the chest wall.

00:03:45.465 --> 00:03:48.830
The model shows the arrangement,
not every microscopic detail.

00:03:49.206 --> 00:03:51.375
Watch this sealed bottle lung model.

00:03:51.704 --> 00:03:55.148
Balloons stand for lungs, a
tube for the airway, and a

00:03:55.148 --> 00:03:56.917
flexible sheet for the diaphragm.

00:03:57.511 --> 00:03:58.743
I pull the sheet down.

00:03:59.277 --> 00:04:00.067
What happens?

00:04:00.831 --> 00:04:04.490
The balloons expand, even though
nobody blows into the tube.

00:04:05.258 --> 00:04:07.472
The space inside the
bottle becomes larger.

00:04:07.865 --> 00:04:10.921
Pressure inside falls below
the outside air pressure.

00:04:11.301 --> 00:04:14.570
Air moves into the balloons from
higher pressure to lower pressure.

00:04:15.378 --> 00:04:15.643
So

00:04:15.675 --> 00:04:18.393
bigger space, lower pressure, air in.

00:04:18.922 --> 00:04:21.996
I was going to say
bigger lungs means higher

00:04:21.996 --> 00:04:22.547
pressure.

00:04:23.323 --> 00:04:24.730
That is our exam trap.

00:04:25.251 --> 00:04:28.865
Follow the cause in order: muscle
action changes chest volume,

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.390
pressure changes, air moves.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.103
Don't start with the air
and forget the muscles.

00:04:34.896 --> 00:04:38.667
In a real person, does someone
pull the diaphragm from underneath?

00:04:39.414 --> 00:04:39.783
No.
